{"name":"Sparkling River","hex":"#d6edf1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sparkling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of sparkle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, reflecting light as if giving off tiny sparks or flashes of light.","Of a beverage, especially an alcoholic beverage, containing dissolved carbon dioxide (either naturally or that has been added) that comes out of solution in the form of many tiny bubbles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sparkling"},{"term":"river","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large and often winding stream which drains a land mass, carrying water down from higher areas to a lower point, oftentimes ending in another body of water, such as an ocean or in an inland sea.","Any large flow of a liquid in a single body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve one’s hand to beat another player on the final card in a poker game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sparkling","blocks":[{"text":"Sparkling is an optical phenomenon exhibited by materials, both solids and liquids, in which numerous points of light appear and disappear, or change in brightness as the material, observer, or light source moves, creating an impression of brilliance and visual movement. In many materials, this effect results from specular reflection by small reflective particles, facets, or flakes that direct incident light toward the observer, although sparkling can also result from refraction and other optical phenomena."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Preferential_concentration.gif/330px-Preferential_concentration.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparkling"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif/250px-Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Drinking_glass_00118.gif/250px-Drinking_glass_00118.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bubbles create the sparkling effect in sparkling water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrinking_glass_00118.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg/500px-Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg/1280px-Sea_otter_and_sun_glitter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w